<div class="docs-rendering-page">

    <div class="docs-eyebrow">ENGINE / GRAPHICS</div>

    <h1>
        Rendering <span class="docs-accent">pipeline</span>
    </h1>

    <p class="docs-lead">
        The current rendering path lives in CraftyNative and submits
        world, entity, sky, HUD, and UI work through Vulcan's graphics
        abstractions.
    </p>

    <h2>Device and swapchain initialization</h2>

    <p>
        <code>CraftyNative.Rendering.CraftyNative.Initialize</code>
        receives the native window and creates a graphics device through
        <code>Vulcan.Vulcan.CreateDevice</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        It initializes the device, creates a swapchain and depth texture,
        command queue and command buffer, then creates mesh/render
        resources and initializes sky, HUD, UI, text, and sprite renderers.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-info">
        <i class="bi bi-info-circle"></i>

        <div>
            <strong>Current graphics backend</strong>

            <p>
                The current implementation uses <code>Vulcan.DirectX</code>
                and explicitly casts command buffer, swapchain, and texture
                objects to D3D11-specific types to obtain render-target
                and depth-stencil views. This is the implementation
                currently checked in—not a claim that all graphics
                backends are supported.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Frame sequence</h2>

    <ol class="docs-steps">
        <li>
            <strong>Begin command buffer</strong>
            <p>
                Set viewport and scissor, clear color and depth targets.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Build camera matrices</strong>
            <p>
                Read the camera object's Transform and Camera components
                and construct view/projection matrices.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Render environment</strong>
            <p>
                Draw the sky using camera position and view-projection data.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Opaque and cutout pass</strong>
            <p>
                Update world batches, draw opaque world sections, then
                draw scene renderables.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Translucent pass</strong>
            <p>
                Draw translucent world sections far-to-near with
                blending enabled and depth writes disabled.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>HUD and UI</strong>
            <p>
                Render HUD, UI elements, text, sprites, and overlays.
            </p>
        </li>

        <li>
            <strong>Present</strong>
            <p>
                End the command buffer and present the swapchain.
            </p>
        </li>
    </ol>

    <h2>World batches and mesh data</h2>

    <p>
        World section meshes are managed through
        <code>WorldMeshManager</code> and drawn by
        <code>WorldBatchRenderer</code>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Individual scene renderables are stored on the scene and drawn
        with their Transform. The mesh renderer handles mesh draw calls
        and resource release.
    </p>

    <h2>Lighting and environment</h2>

    <p>
        Sky, sun, and moon have dedicated environment renderers.
        The runtime exposes daylight and moonlight values.
    </p>

    <p>
        The world shader path uses per-vertex light multiplied by an
        ambient value derived from those environment values. The README
        describes separate opaque/cutout and translucent passes, with
        translucent sections sorted from far to near.
    </p>

    <h2>Shader implementation</h2>

    <p>
        HLSL shader source is embedded in <code>CraftyNative.cs</code>
        in this snapshot. Renderer resources and pipeline descriptions
        are created in the runtime.
    </p>

    <p>
        Vulcan provides the lower-level graphics device, buffers,
        shader and pipeline abstractions.
    </p>

    <div class="docs-callout docs-callout-warning">
        <i class="bi bi-shield-lock"></i>

        <div>
            <strong>Rendering is not exposed by IModContext</strong>

            <p>
                The current mod context exposes only a logger, block
                registry, item registry, and mob registry. It does not expose the
                graphics device, command buffer, mesh renderer, or
                render hooks. Treat these as engine internals.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

